                <pb facs="0931" n="921" sameAs="frus:frus1948v08/pg921"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1115/12–848: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1948-12-08T02:00:00+08:00">December 8, 1948—2 a.m. [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">p.m.?</hi>]</date><lb/>[Received 4:23
                        a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2452. [To Shanghai:] ComNavWesPac informs us that he is directing Admiral
                    Crawford coordinate with you on revision approved Shanghai evacuation plans “to
                    meet essential requirements emphasizing importance initiating action prior
                    outbreak widespread action”. <pb facs="0932" n="922" xml:id="pg922"/> In
                    participating in conference for revision this plan you should be guided by fact
                    that Ambassador or his designated representative has sole authority to determine
                    when situation requires implementation of plan including evacuation and
                    protective features and that Navy or other agencies concerned with carrying out
                    plan should not initiate action without prior instructions from Ambassador or
                    his representative. In Shanghai area you are Ambassador’s “designated
                    representative”.</p>
                <p>Sent Shanghai 1231, repeated Department.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
